window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-health-disease","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Human Health and Disease · Practice","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are mentioned as factors that affect human health?\n(a) Defects a child inherits from parents\n(b) Infections\n(c) Food and water we take\n(d) Rest and exercise given to the body\n(e) Habits that we have or lack\n(f) Proportion of blackbile relative to other humors","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Health is affected by genetic disorders (a), infections (b) and lifestyle, which covers food and water, rest and exercise, and habits (c, d, e). That makes five factors. The humor proportion (f) belongs to a disproved hypothesis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Rejection of a transplanted organ by the recipient's body is mainly due to:","image":"","options":["Humoral immune response produced by B-lymphocytes","Cell-mediated immune response produced by T-lymphocytes","Interferons released by the graft cells","Histamine released from mast cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The body distinguishes 'self' from 'non-self', and the cell-mediated immune response, carried out by T-lymphocytes, is responsible for graft rejection.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a non-ionising radiation that can damage DNA and lead to neoplastic transformation of cells?","image":"","options":["X-rays","Gamma rays","Ultraviolet rays","Cosmic rays"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT names UV as a non-ionising radiation that causes DNA damage leading to cancer. X-rays and gamma rays are carcinogens too, but they are ionising radiations.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: More and more children in metro cities of India are suffering from allergies and asthma due to sensitivity to the environment.\nStatement II: A possible reason is the protected environment provided early in life, which is linked to lowered immunity and greater sensitivity to allergens.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT notes the rise of allergies and asthma among metro children. It suggests this could be due to the protected environment early in life, so both statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"In the term AIDS, the word 'Acquired' indicates that the immune deficiency:","image":"","options":["is inherited from parents and present at birth","develops during the lifetime of an individual and is not congenital","is caused only by acquiring bacterial infections","results from acquiring antibodies from another person"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"'Acquired' means the deficiency of the immune system develops during a person's lifetime, so AIDS is not a congenital disease. Mother-to-child transmission through the placenta is still an infection, not an inherited genetic defect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Heroin ('smack') is chemically diacetylmorphine. How is it obtained?","image":"","options":["By acetylation of morphine extracted from the latex of Papaver somniferum","From the inflorescences of Cannabis sativa","From the leaves of Erythroxylum coca","From the seeds of Datura"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Morphine is extracted from the latex of the opium poppy, and its acetylation gives heroin, an opioid depressant. Cannabis gives cannabinoids, Erythroxylum coca gives cocaine, and Datura is a hallucinogenic plant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A patient receives a kidney from a donor after careful tissue matching and blood group matching. Doctors still advise the patient to take immunosuppressants for life. The best explanation is that:","image":"","options":["Matching completely removes the risk of rejection, but immunosuppressants are needed to prevent bacterial infections","The recipient's body can still recognise the graft as non-self, and its cell-mediated response could reject it","Antibodies in the donor kidney will attack the recipient's own organs unless suppressed","Immunosuppressants are required to increase antibody production against the transplanted tissue"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Even after matching, the recipient's immune system can distinguish self from non-self. T-cell-mediated (cell-mediated) immunity may reject the graft sooner or later, so lifelong immunosuppression is needed. Matching reduces the risk but does not eliminate it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A patient has had a sustained high fever of about $39^\\circ$C to $40^\\circ$C for a week, along with weakness, stomach pain, constipation and loss of appetite. The doctor suspects typhoid. Which test is used to confirm typhoid fever?","image":"","options":["ELISA","Widal test","Biopsy with histopathology","Skin test with small doses of allergens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Typhoid fever, caused by Salmonella typhi, is confirmed by the Widal test. ELISA is the diagnostic test for AIDS, biopsy is used to detect cancer, and allergen testing is used to find the cause of an allergy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (disease) with List-II (mode of transmission):\nList-I\n(A) Amoebiasis\n(B) Filariasis\n(C) Pneumonia\n(D) Ringworm\nList-II\n(I) Inhaling droplets/aerosols or sharing glasses and utensils with an infected person\n(II) Houseflies carrying the parasite from faeces to food\n(III) Soil, or using towels and combs of infected individuals\n(IV) Bite of female mosquito vectors","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Houseflies act as mechanical carriers of Entamoeba, and Wuchereria is spread by female mosquitoes. Pneumonia spreads through droplets or shared utensils. The ringworm fungi are picked up from soil or from shared towels, clothes and combs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Three patients visit a health centre:\nP has dry, scaly, itchy lesions between the toes.\nQ has anaemia, internal bleeding and blockage of the intestinal passage.\nR has a slowly developing, chronic swelling of one lower limb.\nWhich option correctly gives the type of pathogen and the most appropriate preventive measure for each patient's disease?","image":"","options":["P: fungus, avoid sharing towels and combs; Q: helminth, consume clean water and washed vegetables; R: helminth, use mosquito nets and wire mesh","P: fungus, use mosquito nets; Q: protozoan, avoid sharing towels; R: helminth, drink clean water","P: bacterium, avoid sharing towels; Q: helminth, use wire mesh on windows; R: fungus, keep skin dry","P: fungus, avoid sharing towels and combs; Q: helminth, use mosquito nets; R: protozoan, consume clean water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P has ringworm, a fungal disease spread through soil and shared towels, clothes or combs. Q has ascariasis, a helminth infection acquired from contaminated water and food. R has filariasis, a helminth disease spread by female mosquitoes, so the key prevention is avoiding mosquito bites.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Among non-infectious diseases, which one is described as the major cause of death in humans?","image":"","options":["AIDS","Cancer","Malaria","Typhoid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cancer is the major killer among non-infectious diseases. AIDS is also fatal, but it is an infectious disease, so it is the trap option. Malaria and typhoid are infectious too.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A pathogen that enters the human gut must be able to survive the low pH of the stomach and resist the various digestive enzymes.\nStatement II: Most parasites are not considered pathogens, because they live inside the host rather than outside it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pathogens must adapt to the host environment, such as the stomach's acidity and digestive enzymes. Most parasites are in fact pathogens, because they harm the host by living in or on it. Statement II is therefore wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which class of antibodies is produced in response to allergens such as pollen and dust mites?","image":"","options":["IgG","IgA","IgE","IgM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The antibodies produced against allergens belong to the IgE type. They trigger mast cells to release chemicals such as histamine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the life cycle of Plasmodium is correct?","image":"","options":["Sporozoites enter the human body through the bite of an infected male Anopheles mosquito","The parasites first multiply in red blood cells and later move into liver cells","Rupture of RBCs releases haemozoin, which causes chill and high fever recurring every three to four days","Sporozoites formed in the mosquito are stored in its gut until the next bite"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Haemozoin released on rupture of RBCs causes the recurring chill and fever. The vector is the female Anopheles. Multiplication occurs first in liver cells and then in RBCs. Sporozoites are stored in the mosquito's salivary glands, not its gut.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A 15-year-old athlete secretly uses anabolic steroids for two years to build muscle bulk. Besides severe facial and body acne, he is likely to end up shorter than expected. According to NCERT, what is the reason for this stunted growth?","image":"","options":["Delayed closure of the growth centres of long bones","Premature closure of the growth centres of the long bones","Enlargement of the prostate gland diverting nutrients from bones","Reduced testicular size lowering sperm production"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In adolescents, anabolic steroids cause premature closure of the growth centres of long bones, which stops further lengthening and results in stunted growth. Prostate enlargement and reduced testicular size are real male side effects but do not explain the reduced height.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following organisms is a bacterium responsible for pneumonia in humans?","image":"","options":["Haemophilus influenzae","Rhinovirus","Salmonella typhi","Entamoeba histolytica"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Haemophilus influenzae and Streptococcus pneumoniae are the bacteria that cause pneumonia. Despite its name, H. influenzae is a bacterium and not a virus. Salmonella typhi causes typhoid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Mary Mallon, nicknamed 'Typhoid Mary', is a classic case in medicine because she:","image":"","options":["discovered Salmonella typhi as the causative agent of typhoid","developed the Widal test for confirming typhoid","was the first person to recover from typhoid after antibiotic treatment","was a cook and typhoid carrier who spread the disease for years through the food she prepared"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Mary Mallon was a cook who carried typhoid and kept spreading it for several years through food. Her case shows how a carrier can transmit a food-borne disease.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which lymphoid organ filters the blood by trapping blood-borne micro-organisms and also holds a large reservoir of erythrocytes?","image":"","options":["Thymus","Bone marrow","Spleen","Appendix"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The spleen is bean-shaped and contains mainly lymphocytes and phagocytes. It filters blood-borne microbes and stores a large reserve of RBCs. Bone marrow produces blood cells, but it is not described as a filter of blood.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Cells infected by a virus release certain proteins that protect neighbouring non-infected cells from further viral infection. These proteins and the type of innate barrier they represent are:","image":"","options":["Antibodies; physiological barrier","Interferons; cytokine barrier","Histamines; cellular barrier","Interferons; physical barrier"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Virus-infected cells secrete interferons, which protect uninfected cells; NCERT classifies them as the cytokine barrier of innate immunity. Antibodies belong to acquired immunity, not innate barriers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An infant fed on colostrum receives passive immunity, because colostrum contains abundant IgA antibodies made by the mother.\nStatement II: Hepatitis B vaccine produced from yeast by recombinant DNA technology gives passive immunity, because the antigen is made outside the human body.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Colostrum supplies ready-made antibodies, so it gives passive immunity. A vaccine delivers antigenic polypeptides, and the recipient's own body makes antibodies and memory cells, so it gives active immunity no matter where the antigen was produced.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"An antibody molecule is represented as $H_2L_2$. This notation indicates that each antibody molecule has:","image":"","options":["Two heavy chains and two light chains, four peptide chains in all","Two hydrophobic and two lipid-soluble domains","Two long light chains and two short heavy chains","Four peptide chains, all of equal length"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each antibody has four peptide chains: two longer heavy (H) chains and two smaller light (L) chains, hence $H_2L_2$. The trap is reversing which chains are long and which are short.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are bacterial diseases of humans?\nTyphoid, Plague, Common cold, Diphtheria, Ringworm, Pneumonia, Amoebiasis","image":"","options":["3","5","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Typhoid, plague, diphtheria and pneumonia are bacterial diseases, which makes four. The common cold is viral, ringworm is fungal and amoebiasis is protozoan.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"According to the early 'humor' concept of health held by Hippocrates and the Ayurveda system, persons with 'blackbile' were believed to:","image":"","options":["Have a cold personality and suffer from chills","Have a hot personality and suffer from fevers","Have a calm personality and be immune to infections","Have normal body temperature and perfect health"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The humor hypothesis linked blackbile to a hot personality and fevers. Thermometer readings later showed these persons actually had a normal body temperature.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A person who is physically fit and free from any disease is necessarily healthy","Good health increases longevity and reduces infant and maternal mortality","Diseases that are easily transmitted from one person to another are called infectious diseases","Disease is indicated when the functioning of one or more organs or systems is adversely affected, with appearance of signs and symptoms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Health is not just the absence of disease or physical fitness. A person who is fit and disease-free may still lack mental or social well-being. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person who repeatedly uses a drug soon needs larger doses to obtain the same effect.\nReason (R): With repeated use of drugs, the tolerance level of the receptors in the body increases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"As receptor tolerance rises, the receptors respond only to higher doses, so intake increases and leads to addiction. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Rejection of a transplanted organ is mediated mainly by antibodies secreted by B-lymphocytes.\nReason (R): The body is able to differentiate between 'self' and 'non-self'.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Graft rejection is caused by the cell-mediated immune response, which is carried out by T-lymphocytes, not by the antibody response. The self/non-self discrimination stated in R is true.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Normal cells stop dividing uncontrollably when they touch neighbouring cells. Which property is lost in cancer cells, so that they keep dividing and form tumors?","image":"","options":["Metastasis","Contact inhibition","Phagocytosis","Cell differentiation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In normal cells, contact with other cells inhibits uncontrolled growth (contact inhibition). Cancer cells lose this property and form tumors. Metastasis is a property that malignant cells gain, not one they lose.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) makes up approximately what proportion of the total lymphoid tissue in the human body?","image":"","options":["About 10 per cent","About 25 per cent","About 50 per cent","About 90 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"MALT lies within the lining of the respiratory, digestive and urogenital tracts. It makes up about half (50%) of the body's lymphoid tissue.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are warning signs of drug and alcohol abuse among youth as described in NCERT?\n(i) Drop in academic performance\n(ii) Unexplained absence from school/college\n(iii) Lack of interest in personal hygiene\n(iv) Increased interest in hobbies\n(v) Fluctuations in weight and appetite\n(vi) Improving relationships with family and friends\n(vii) Change in sleeping and eating habits","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Five items are warning signs: (i), (ii), (iii), (v) and (vii). NCERT actually lists loss of interest in hobbies and deteriorating relationships as warning signs, so (iv) and (vi) are the reverse of what it describes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are physiological barriers of innate immunity?\nAcid in the stomach, saliva in the mouth, skin, tears from the eyes, mucus coating of the respiratory epithelium, interferons","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Stomach acid, saliva and tears are physiological barriers. Skin and the mucus coating are physical barriers, and interferons form the cytokine barrier.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Heroin, commonly called 'smack', is chemically best described as:","image":"","options":["Diacetylmorphine, obtained by acetylation of morphine extracted from the latex of Papaver somniferum","Diacetylmorphine, obtained by acetylation of an alkaloid extracted from the leaves of Erythroxylum coca","Morphine, obtained directly without modification from the latex of Papaver somniferum","A cannabinoid obtained by acetylation of the resin of Cannabis sativa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Heroin is a white, odourless, bitter crystalline compound made by acetylating morphine, which comes from the latex of the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum. Erythroxylum coca is the source of cocaine, not heroin.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Entamoeba histolytica, the causative agent of amoebic dysentery, is a parasite of the human","image":"","options":["small intestine","liver cells","lymphatic vessels","large intestine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan that lives in the large intestine and causes amoebiasis. Do not confuse it with Salmonella typhi, which enters through the small intestine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Allergen\n(b) Mast cells\n(c) Rheumatoid arthritis\n(d) Colostrum\nList-II\n(i) Rich in IgA antibodies\n(ii) Release histamine and serotonin\n(iii) Mites in dust\n(iv) Body attacks its own cells\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Dust mites are a common allergen, and mast cells release histamine and serotonin during allergic reactions. Rheumatoid arthritis is an auto-immune disease in which the body attacks self-cells. Colostrum is rich in IgA antibodies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are secondary lymphoid organs?\nSpleen, Thymus, Tonsils, Bone marrow, Peyer's patches, Appendix, Lymph nodes","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Spleen, tonsils, Peyer's patches, appendix and lymph nodes are secondary lymphoid organs, which makes five. Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs, where lymphocytes mature.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Proteins called interferons, secreted by virus-infected cells, belong to which type of innate immune barrier?","image":"","options":["Physical barrier","Physiological barrier","Cellular barrier","Cytokine barrier"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Interferons are proteins released by virus-infected cells that protect neighbouring non-infected cells from viral infection. They therefore form the cytokine barrier. They are not cells, so they are not a cellular barrier.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which age range may be thought of as the period of adolescence?","image":"","options":["8–12 years","12–18 years","18–24 years","10–14 years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT treats 12–18 years as the adolescence period. It describes this phase as the bridge between childhood and adulthood and a vulnerable stage of mental and psychological development.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Heroin is a stimulant that speeds up body functions.\nStatement II: Heroin is chemically diacetylmorphine, obtained by acetylation of morphine extracted from the latex of Papaver somniferum.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Heroin (smack) is diacetylmorphine derived from poppy latex, so Statement II is correct. Heroin is a depressant that slows down body functions, so Statement I is incorrect. Cocaine is the stimulant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Proteins that are secreted by virus-infected cells and protect non-infected cells from further viral infection belong to which category of innate immunity barrier?","image":"","options":["Physical barrier","Physiological barrier","Cellular barrier","Cytokine barrier"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"These proteins are interferons, which form the cytokine barrier of innate immunity. Interferons are proteins, so they should not be confused with cellular barriers such as neutrophils, monocytes, NK cells and macrophages.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Rheumatoid arthritis is an example of which type of condition?","image":"","options":["Allergy caused by IgE","Immunodeficiency acquired through a viral infection","Auto-immune disease in which the body attacks its own cells","Bacterial infection of the joints"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In rheumatoid arthritis the body attacks its own (self) cells. NCERT gives it as its example of an auto-immune disease. It is neither an allergy nor an infection.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the immune system is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Natural killer cells are lymphocytes, yet they are counted among the barriers of innate immunity","The response to a second encounter with the same pathogen is highly intensified because of memory of the first encounter","T-lymphocytes do not secrete antibodies, so they have no role in the humoral immune response","Humoral immunity is so named because the antibodies involved are found in the blood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"T-cells do not secrete antibodies, but they help B-cells produce them, so they do contribute to the humoral response. The other statements match NCERT: NK cells are an innate cellular barrier, the secondary (anamnestic) response is intensified, and antibody-mediated immunity is called humoral.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A young man snorts a white powdered drug and soon reports a strong sense of euphoria and increased energy. On a later occasion, after taking a much larger dose, he experiences hallucinations. Which option correctly identifies the mode of action and the source of this drug?","image":"","options":["Binds opioid receptors in the CNS; obtained from latex of Papaver somniferum","Interacts with cannabinoid receptors in the brain; obtained from inflorescences of Cannabis sativa","Stimulates the adrenal gland to release adrenaline; obtained from tobacco leaves","Interferes with dopamine transport; obtained from Erythroxylum coca"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The clues point to cocaine: it is snorted, it is a CNS stimulant causing euphoria and energy, and excessive doses cause hallucinations. Cocaine comes from Erythroxylum coca and interferes with dopamine transport. Heroin is also snorted, but it is a depressant, which rules out the opioid option.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A patient presents with fever, chills, cough and headache. A chest examination shows fluid in the alveoli, and the finger nails have a bluish tinge. Which option correctly pairs the likely causative organism with a mode by which the patient could have acquired it?","image":"","options":["Rhinovirus; touching contaminated doorknobs and computer keyboards","Salmonella typhi; consuming contaminated food and water","Streptococcus pneumoniae; inhaling droplets from an infected person or sharing utensils","Haemophilus influenzae; houseflies carrying the pathogen from faeces to food"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fluid-filled alveoli with bluish nails indicate pneumonia, which is acquired by inhaling droplets or aerosols or by sharing utensils. Rhinoviruses do not infect the lungs, and S. typhi is food- and water-borne. Houseflies as mechanical carriers are linked to amoebiasis, not to pneumonia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Fishes like Gambusia are introduced into ponds as a public health measure because they:","image":"","options":["Feed on mosquito larvae and so reduce vector populations","Feed on Ascaris eggs present in contaminated water","Secrete chemicals that kill Plasmodium in water","Feed on houseflies that carry Entamoeba cysts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Gambusia feeds on mosquito larvae. This is a biological way of controlling the mosquito vectors of diseases like malaria and filariasis. It has no role against helminth eggs or houseflies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A patient with prolonged fever, weight loss and recurrent infections is suspected to have AIDS. Which of the following is a widely used diagnostic test for this condition?","image":"","options":["Widal test","ELISA","Biopsy with histopathology","Computed tomography"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Enzyme linked immuno-sorbent assay (ELISA) is the widely used diagnostic test for AIDS. The Widal test confirms typhoid, while biopsy and CT are used in cancer detection.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A healthy person acquires ascariasis through the bite of an infected female mosquito.\n(ii) In filariasis, the genital organs are often affected, resulting in gross deformities.\n(iii) Ringworm fungi thrive in skin folds such as the groin because heat and moisture favour their growth.\n(iv) Smallpox has been completely eradicated through vaccination, whereas polio, diphtheria and tetanus have been controlled to a large extent.\n(v) Internal bleeding and anaemia are among the symptoms of ascariasis.\n(vi) Dengue and chikungunya are transmitted by Anopheles mosquitoes.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (ii), (iii), (iv) and (v) are correct. Ascariasis spreads through water, vegetables and fruits contaminated with eggs from faeces, not through a mosquito bite. Dengue and chikungunya are transmitted by Aedes, not Anopheles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Rejection of a transplanted organ is mainly brought about by the humoral (antibody-mediated) immune response produced by B-lymphocytes.\nStatement II: Even after tissue matching and blood group matching, a transplant recipient usually has to take immunosuppressants throughout life.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Graft rejection is due to the cell-mediated immune response mediated by T-lymphocytes, which distinguish self from non-self. So Statement I is wrong. Lifelong immunosuppressants are still needed after matching, so Statement II is right.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Hippocrates and Ayurveda\n(B) William Harvey\n(C) Thermometer\n(D) Neural and endocrine systems\nList-II\n(I) Discovery of blood circulation by the experimental method\n(II) Showed normal body temperature in persons with blackbile\n(III) Link through which the mind influences the immune system\n(IV) Humor hypothesis arrived at by pure reflective thought","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The humor idea of Hippocrates and Ayurveda came from reflective thought (IV). Harvey used experiments to discover blood circulation (I). Thermometer readings showed normal temperature in persons with blackbile (II). The neural and endocrine systems link the mind to immunity (III).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following act as cellular barriers of innate immunity that can phagocytose and destroy microbes?\nPolymorphonuclear leukocytes (neutrophils), monocytes, natural killer cells, macrophages in tissues, interferons, mucus","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four cellular barriers: neutrophils, monocytes, natural killer cells and tissue macrophages. Interferons are a cytokine barrier and mucus is a physical barrier.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cancer cells have lost contact inhibition and therefore keep dividing to form masses of cells called tumors.\nStatement II: Benign tumors shed cells that travel through the blood and start new tumors at distant sites.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct because loss of contact inhibition leads to uncontrolled division and tumor formation. Statement II is wrong: spread to distant sites (metastasis) is a property of malignant tumors, while benign tumors stay confined to their original location.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about cancer are correct?\n(a) Proto-oncogenes are present in normal cells and can cause oncogenic transformation when activated under certain conditions.\n(b) UV radiation, being non-ionising, cannot cause neoplastic transformation of cells.\n(c) Computed tomography uses strong magnetic fields to generate a three-dimensional image of internal organs.\n(d) Malignant tumor cells starve normal cells by competing with them for vital nutrients.\n(e) The majority of chemotherapeutic drugs have side effects such as hair loss and anemia.\n(f) Cells of benign tumors commonly reach distant sites through blood and form new tumors.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (d) and (e) are correct, so three statements are true. UV is non-ionising but still damages DNA and can cause neoplastic transformation. CT uses X-rays, whereas MRI uses magnetic fields. Spread through blood (metastasis) is a property of malignant tumors, not benign ones.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about HIV/AIDS are correct?\n(i) HIV is a retrovirus having an envelope that encloses an RNA genome.\n(ii) Reverse transcriptase helps form viral DNA from the viral RNA.\n(iii) HIV rapidly kills infected macrophages, which limits further virus production.\n(iv) The time-lag between infection and appearance of AIDS symptoms is usually 5–10 years.\n(v) ELISA is a widely used diagnostic test for AIDS.\n(vi) Anti-retroviral drugs completely cure AIDS.\n(vii) AIDS is a congenital immunodeficiency.","image":"","options":["5","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iv) and (v) are correct, which makes four. Infected macrophages keep producing virus and act as an 'HIV factory', anti-retroviral drugs only prolong life, and 'acquired' means AIDS is not congenital.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"The chill and high fever that recur every three to four days in malaria are due to the release of which toxic substance?","image":"","options":["Histamine","Interferon","Haemozoin","Serotonin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When Plasmodium-infected RBCs rupture, they release the toxin haemozoin, which causes the recurring chill and fever. Histamine and serotonin are linked to allergy, and interferons are antiviral proteins.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Salmonella typhi enters the small intestine through contaminated food and water and reaches other organs through blood","A healthy person can acquire pneumonia by sharing glasses and utensils with an infected person","Intestinal perforation leading to death may occur in severe cases of pneumonia","Symptoms of the common cold usually last for 3–7 days"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Intestinal perforation is a complication of severe typhoid, not of pneumonia. Pneumonia affects the alveoli of the lungs. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Proto-oncogenes are present in normal cells and, when activated under certain conditions, can lead to oncogenic transformation.\nStatement II: Cancer patients may be given α-interferon, a biological response modifier that activates their immune system to help destroy tumors.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cellular oncogenes (c-onc), also called proto-oncogenes, occur in normal cells and can cause cancer when activated. Tumor cells tend to evade the immune system, so α-interferon is given to boost the patient's immune response.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the basis of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) used in cancer detection?","image":"","options":["X-rays used to generate a three-dimensional image","Gamma rays that lethally irradiate tumor cells","Strong magnetic fields and non-ionising radiations","Radio-labelled antibodies against cancer-specific antigens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"MRI uses strong magnetic fields and non-ionising radiations to detect pathological and physiological changes in living tissue. The X-ray based three-dimensional imaging technique is CT, not MRI.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For filariasis, the most important preventive measure is to control or eliminate mosquitoes and their breeding places.\nReason (R): Eggs of Wuchereria are passed out in the faeces of infected persons and contaminate drinking water.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true because filariasis is transmitted by female mosquito vectors, which makes vector control the key measure. R is false since faecal eggs contaminating soil and water describe Ascaris, not Wuchereria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Reverse transcriptase\n(B) Toxoplasma\n(C) NACO\n(D) Anti-retroviral drugs\nList-II\n(I) Organisation educating people about AIDS in India\n(II) Prolong the life of an AIDS patient but cannot prevent death\n(III) Forms viral DNA from the RNA genome of HIV\n(IV) Parasite causing infection in an immuno-deficient patient\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pairs are as follows:\n- Reverse transcriptase copies the viral RNA into DNA.\n- Toxoplasma is a parasite that infects patients whose helper T cells are depleted.\n- NACO is the National AIDS Control Organisation, which educates people.\n- Anti-retroviral drugs are only partially effective: they prolong life but cannot cure AIDS.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Heroin\nB. Cannabinoids\nC. Cocaine\nD. Nicotine\nList-II\nI. Interferes with transport of the neurotransmitter dopamine\nII. Stimulates adrenal gland to release adrenaline and nor-adrenaline\nIII. Obtained by acetylation of morphine; a depressant\nIV. Obtained from inflorescences of Cannabis sativa; affects cardiovascular system\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Heroin is diacetylmorphine and acts as a depressant. Cannabinoids come from Cannabis inflorescences and affect the cardiovascular system. Cocaine interferes with dopamine transport, and nicotine stimulates release of adrenaline and nor-adrenaline.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following are side effects of the majority of chemotherapeutic drugs used against cancer?","image":"","options":["Hair loss and anemia","Masculinisation and deepening of voice","Hallucinations and euphoria","Graft rejection and allergy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Most chemotherapeutic drugs that kill cancer cells have side effects such as hair loss and anemia. Masculinisation is a side effect of anabolic steroid misuse, and hallucinations are caused by drugs such as cocaine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cancer patients are often given α-interferon as part of their treatment.\nReason (R): Tumor cells can avoid detection and destruction by the immune system, and α-interferon acts as a biological response modifier that activates the immune system.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Tumor cells evade the immune system. α-interferon, a biological response modifier, activates the patient's immunity so it helps destroy the tumor. R therefore directly explains why α-interferon is given.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the common cold is correct?","image":"","options":["It is caused by bacteria such as Haemophilus influenzae.","Rhinoviruses infect the nose and respiratory passage but not the lungs.","Its symptoms usually last for 3-4 weeks.","It spreads only through direct inhalation of droplets and never through objects."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rhinoviruses cause the common cold and infect the nose and respiratory passage but not the lungs. Symptoms last about 3-7 days. Infection also spreads through contaminated objects such as pens, cups and doorknobs. H. influenzae is a bacterium that causes pneumonia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person with AIDS commonly suffers from infections caused by Mycobacterium and Toxoplasma.\nReason (R): HIV is a retrovirus having an envelope enclosing an RNA genome.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true. These opportunistic infections occur because helper T-lymphocytes progressively decline, not because of the structure of the virus. So R does not explain A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cancer patients are given $\\alpha$-interferon as part of immunotherapy.\nReason (R): $\\alpha$-interferon is secreted by tumor cells to help them escape detection by the immune system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true because $\\alpha$-interferon is given as a biological response modifier. R is false: tumor cells avoid immune destruction on their own, and $\\alpha$-interferon is a therapy that activates the patient's immune system against the tumor. It is not something tumor cells secrete.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In severe cases of pneumonia, the lips and finger nails of the patient may turn gray to bluish in colour.\nReason (R): In pneumonia, the alveoli get filled with fluid, which causes severe problems in respiration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Fluid-filled alveoli impair gas exchange, so oxygenation of the blood falls. This shows up as a gray-bluish colour of the lips and nails, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Entamoeba histolytica, the causative agent of amoebic dysentery, lives as a parasite in which part of the human body?","image":"","options":["Small intestine","Large intestine","Liver cells","Lymphatic vessels of lower limbs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan parasite of the human large intestine. The small intestine is the entry site of Salmonella typhi, and lymphatic vessels are where Wuchereria lives.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Ringworm infections are frequently found in the groin and between the toes. The best explanation for this is that:","image":"","options":["These regions lack skin, so the fungi enter directly","Heat and moisture in skin folds favour the growth of the causative fungi","Mosquito vectors preferentially bite these regions","Lymphatic vessels of these regions harbour the fungi for many years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Heat and moisture help ringworm fungi grow, so they thrive in skin folds such as the groin and between the toes. The disease is not vector-borne, and residence in lymphatic vessels describes filarial worms, not ringworm fungi.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Metastasis\n(b) Contact inhibition\n(c) Proto-oncogene\n(d) Biological response modifier\nList-II\n(i) Gene in normal cells that can cause oncogenic transformation when activated\n(ii) Spread of cells sloughed from a tumor to distant sites through blood\n(iii) Substance such as $\\alpha$-interferon that activates the patient's immune system\n(iv) Property of normal cells that restricts their uncontrolled growth\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Metastasis is the spread of sloughed tumor cells through blood. Contact inhibition restricts the growth of normal cells. Proto-oncogenes (c-onc) are normal genes that can cause transformation once activated. $\\alpha$-interferon is a biological response modifier.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events after HIV infection in the correct sequence:\n(a) Viral DNA is incorporated into the host cell's DNA\n(b) Progeny viruses repeatedly attack helper T-lymphocytes, progressively reducing their number\n(c) Virus enters macrophages and its RNA genome is converted to DNA\n(d) Person suffers infections by Mycobacterium, fungi and parasites that could otherwise have been overcome\n(e) Infected macrophages continue producing virus particles","image":"","options":["(c) → (a) → (e) → (b) → (d)","(a) → (c) → (e) → (d) → (b)","(c) → (e) → (a) → (b) → (d)","(c) → (a) → (b) → (e) → (d)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct order is as follows:\n1. HIV first enters macrophages, where reverse transcriptase makes viral DNA (c).\n2. This DNA integrates into the host DNA (a).\n3. The macrophages then keep producing virus (e).\n4. HIV progressively depletes helper T cells (b).\n5. Only then do opportunistic infections appear (d).\n\nThe trap in option 3 is placing virus production before integration, which is impossible because the integrated DNA is what directs virus production.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are examples of passive immunity?\n1. IgA received by an infant through colostrum\n2. Antibodies received by the foetus through the placenta\n3. Antitoxin injected into a person at risk of tetanus\n4. Antivenom given after a snakebite\n5. Hepatitis B vaccine produced in yeast\n6. Immunity developed after recovering from a natural infection","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Items 1–4 supply ready-made antibodies, so they are passive immunity. The vaccine (5) and natural infection (6) expose the host to antigens, which then makes its own antibodies. Those two are active immunity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Pathogen X enters the bloodstream directly. Pathogen Y gets into the tissue fluid and lymph through a skin wound. Which secondary lymphoid organs are mainly responsible for trapping X and Y, respectively?","image":"","options":["Lymph nodes and spleen","Spleen and lymph nodes","Thymus and bone marrow","Bone marrow and thymus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The spleen filters blood and traps blood-borne micro-organisms, so it deals with X. Lymph nodes along the lymphatic system trap antigens in lymph and tissue fluid, so they deal with Y. Thymus and bone marrow are primary organs and do not act as antigen traps.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are examples of passive immunity?\n(i) IgA received by a newborn through colostrum\n(ii) Antibodies passing from mother to foetus across the placenta\n(iii) Antitoxin injected into a person infected with tetanus\n(iv) Antivenom injected after a snakebite\n(v) Recombinant hepatitis B vaccine given to a child\n(vi) Recovery from a natural infection that leaves the person protected","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Items (i) to (iv) all supply ready-made antibodies, so they are passive immunity. A vaccine and a natural infection both expose the body to antigens, so the host makes its own antibodies. That makes (v) and (vi) active immunity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cancer detection is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In biopsy, thin stained sections of suspected tissue are examined under a microscope by a pathologist","Leukemias are detected by blood and bone marrow tests for increased cell counts","Computed tomography uses X-rays to generate a three-dimensional image of internal organs","MRI relies on ionising radiations to detect changes in living tissue"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"MRI uses strong magnetic fields and non-ionising radiations, so the last statement is incorrect. The other three statements correctly describe biopsy with histopathology, leukemia tests and CT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (substance) with List-II (feature) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(A) Cocaine\n(B) Charas\n(C) Heroin\n(D) Nicotine\nList-II:\n(I) Interferes with the transport of the neurotransmitter dopamine\n(II) Stimulates the adrenal gland to release adrenaline and nor-adrenaline\n(III) Derived from Cannabis sativa; known for effects on the cardiovascular system\n(IV) Binds opioid receptors in the CNS and GI tract and slows down body functions","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cocaine interferes with dopamine transport, and charas is a cannabis product that acts on the cardiovascular system. Heroin is an opioid depressant, and nicotine triggers release of adrenaline and nor-adrenaline. A common trap is swapping cocaine (a stimulant) with heroin (a depressant).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Houseflies can spread amoebiasis in a locality with poor sanitation.\nReason (R): Entamoeba histolytica multiplies inside the housefly, which acts as its intermediate host.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Houseflies only mechanically carry the parasite from faeces to food, so A is true. The parasite does not multiply or develop inside the fly, so R is false. Contrast this with Anopheles, in which Plasmodium actually develops.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (drug) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(a) Heroin\n(b) Cannabinoids\n(c) Cocaine\n(d) Nicotine\nList-II\n(i) Interferes with the transport of the neurotransmitter dopamine\n(ii) Stimulates the adrenal gland to release adrenaline and nor-adrenaline\n(iii) Interacts with receptors present principally in the brain; known for effects on the cardiovascular system\n(iv) A depressant that slows down body functions\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Heroin (diacetylmorphine) is a depressant. Cannabinoids act on brain receptors and affect the cardiovascular system. Cocaine interferes with dopamine transport, and nicotine makes the adrenal gland release adrenaline and nor-adrenaline. A common trap is to treat heroin as a stimulant like cocaine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person infected with the tetanus pathogen is given an injection of preformed antibodies (antitoxin) rather than relying only on vaccination at that moment.\nReason (R): Active immunity is slow and takes time to give its full effective response.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Tetanus is deadly, so the patient needs immediate protection. Active immunity develops too slowly to help in time, which is why preformed antibodies are given as passive immunisation. R therefore correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"The hepatitis B vaccine made by recombinant DNA technology is produced using which organism?","image":"","options":["Yeast","Attenuated hepatitis B virus grown in chick embryo","Plasmodium cultures","Human helper T-cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Recombinant DNA technology lets antigenic polypeptides of the pathogen be made in bacteria or yeast. The hepatitis B vaccine is made in yeast, which allows large-scale production.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Feeding colostrum to a new-born infant gives it passive immunity.\nReason (R): Colostrum secreted during the initial days of lactation contains abundant IgA antibodies.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The infant receives ready-made IgA antibodies through colostrum, and receiving ready-made antibodies is what defines passive immunity. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Chronic use of drugs and alcohol damages the nervous system and leads to cirrhosis of which organ?","image":"","options":["Kidney","Pancreas","Liver","Lungs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cirrhosis is the liver damage caused by chronic alcohol and drug use. It should not be confused with the kidney dysfunction listed among anabolic steroid side effects.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Parenting that combines high levels of nurturance with consistent discipline is associated with a lowered risk of substance abuse.\nStatement II: Using a drug just once cannot be a forerunner to addiction; only regular use can.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT links nurturing yet disciplined parenting to lower substance abuse risk, so Statement I is correct. NCERT also warns that using drugs even once can be a forerunner to addiction, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which species of Plasmodium causes malignant malaria, the most serious and potentially fatal form of the disease?","image":"","options":["Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium malariae","Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium ovale"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT identifies malignant malaria, caused by P. falciparum, as the most serious type of malaria; it can even be fatal. P. vivax and P. malariae cause other, less severe types.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about malaria is correct?","image":"","options":["Male Anopheles mosquitoes transmit sporozoites to humans while feeding on blood","Sporozoites injected into humans directly attack red blood cells before reaching any other organ","Plasmodium needs two hosts to complete its life cycle, and the female Anopheles is both a host and the vector","The recurring fever of malaria is caused by haemozoin released when infected liver cells burst"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plasmodium completes its life cycle in humans and mosquitoes, and the female Anopheles also acts as the vector. Only females transmit the parasite. The parasite multiplies in liver cells first, and the fever-causing haemozoin comes from ruptured RBCs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following does NOT transmit HIV from an infected person to a healthy person?","image":"","options":["Transfusion of contaminated blood or blood products","Sharing of infected needles by intravenous drug abusers","Hugging or shaking hands with the infected person","Passage from an infected mother to her child through the placenta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"HIV spreads only through body fluids, by sexual contact, contaminated blood, shared needles and across the placenta. It does not spread by mere touch or physical contact. This is why infected persons should not be isolated from family and society.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"The yellowish fluid secreted by the mother in the first few days of lactation protects the newborn mainly because it is rich in which antibody?","image":"","options":["IgE","IgA","IgM","IgD"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Colostrum contains abundant IgA antibodies. These give the infant ready-made protection, which is passive immunity. IgE is the antibody linked to allergy, not colostrum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Bone marrow\n(B) Thymus\n(C) Lymph nodes\n(D) MALT\nList-II\n(i) Reduces to a very small size by puberty\n(ii) Lymphoid tissue within the lining of major body tracts\n(iii) Site where all blood cells are produced\n(iv) Trap antigens that enter lymph and tissue fluid\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Bone marrow is the main lymphoid organ that produces all blood cells. The thymus shrinks with age until puberty. Lymph nodes trap antigens from lymph and tissue fluid, and MALT lines the respiratory, digestive and urogenital tracts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"In an HIV-infected person, which cells keep producing the virus and therefore act like an 'HIV factory'?","image":"","options":["Neutrophils","B-lymphocytes","Erythrocytes","Macrophages"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"HIV first enters macrophages, where its DNA joins the host DNA. The macrophages then go on producing virus particles, which is why they are called an HIV factory. Helper T cells are the cells that get progressively destroyed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Benign tumors normally remain confined to their original location and cause little damage.\nStatement II: Cells of malignant tumors grow rapidly, invade surrounding normal tissues and starve normal cells by competing for vital nutrients.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Both statements match NCERT. Benign tumors stay localised. Malignant (neoplastic) cells proliferate rapidly, invade surrounding tissue and compete with normal cells for nutrients.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A kidney transplant patient must take immunosuppressants throughout life, even after tissue matching and blood group matching with the donor.\nReason (R): The body can distinguish 'self' from 'non-self', and the cell-mediated immune response, carried out by T-lymphocytes, is responsible for graft rejection.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Matching reduces the risk of rejection but does not remove it. T-cell-mediated immunity can still recognise the graft as non-self, so lifelong immunosuppression is needed. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A typhoid carrier working as a cook can keep spreading the disease for several years through the food he or she prepares.\nStatement II: Typhoid is primarily an air-borne disease, so avoiding close contact with infected persons is the main preventive measure.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The Mary Mallon case shows that a carrier spreads typhoid through food. Typhoid is transmitted through contaminated food and water, so hygiene in food, water and public catering is the key measure. Avoiding close contact is emphasised for air-borne diseases such as pneumonia and the common cold.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an example of a physical barrier of innate immunity?","image":"","options":["Acid secreted in the stomach","Tears secreted by the eyes","Mucus coating of the epithelium lining the respiratory tract","Neutrophils circulating in the blood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Skin and the mucus coating of the respiratory, gastrointestinal and urogenital epithelia are physical barriers that trap microbes. Stomach acid and tears are physiological barriers. Neutrophils form a cellular barrier.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the human lymphoid system:\nStatement I: Peyer's patches of the small intestine and the tonsils are secondary lymphoid organs, where mature lymphocytes interact with antigens and proliferate to become effector cells.\nStatement II: The thymus, located near the heart beneath the breastbone, is very small at birth and keeps enlarging until puberty, when it reaches its maximum size.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Peyer's patches and tonsils are secondary lymphoid organs where mature lymphocytes meet antigens and proliferate, so Statement I is correct. Statement II reverses the real pattern: the thymus is quite large at birth and shrinks with age, becoming very small by puberty.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"The 'good humor' hypothesis of health was disproved mainly by which of the following?","image":"","options":["Discovery of antibiotics and the eradication of smallpox by vaccination","William Harvey's discovery of blood circulation by the experimental method, and thermometer readings showing normal body temperature in persons with blackbile","Microscopic observation of pathogens in the blood of persons with blackbile","Ayurvedic texts showing that fevers arise from an excess of phlegm rather than blackbile"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Experimental evidence overturned the humor idea. This was Harvey's work on blood circulation, together with the finding that people with 'blackbile' had a normal body temperature. The humor idea itself had come only from reflective thought.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A person sneezes and has watery eyes every time he visits a house with pet cats, because he reacts to animal dander. Which class of antibodies is mainly produced against such allergens?","image":"","options":["IgA","IgG","IgE","IgM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Allergy is an exaggerated immune response to environmental antigens such as dander, pollen and dust mites. The antibodies produced against allergens are of the IgE type. IgA is the antibody associated with colostrum, not with allergy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In an HIV-infected person, macrophages act like an 'HIV factory'.\nReason (R): Inside the macrophage, the viral RNA genome is copied into viral DNA by reverse transcriptase, and this DNA gets incorporated into the host cell's DNA, directing the cell to produce virus particles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The viral DNA integrates into the host genome, so the macrophage keeps producing new virus particles. That continuous production is exactly why it is called an HIV factory, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy person develops malaria after being bitten by a mosquito. Which of the following must have been true of that mosquito?","image":"","options":["It was a male Anopheles that had sucked plant juices contaminated with parasites","It was a female Anopheles that had earlier fed on an infected person, in whose body the parasites developed into sporozoites stored in its salivary glands","It was an Aedes mosquito that carried haemozoin from an infected person's blood","It was a female Anopheles that injected infected red blood cells taken from a malaria patient a few minutes earlier"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The parasites must first develop and multiply inside a female Anopheles that fed on an infected person, forming sporozoites in its salivary glands. These sporozoites are then injected into the next human it bites. Simply carrying infected RBCs or haemozoin does not transmit malaria, and Aedes transmits dengue and chikungunya.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the immune response is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The response produced by B-cells is called humoral because the antibodies are found in the blood","IgA, IgM, IgE and IgG are some of the different types of antibodies produced in the body","The secondary (anamnestic) response to a pathogen is more intense than the primary response to it","Interferons destroy the viruses present inside the very cells that secrete them, thereby curing those cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Interferons are secreted by virus-infected cells, but they protect other, non-infected cells from further viral infection. They do not cure the infected cell itself. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements regarding the life cycle of Plasmodium are correct?\n(i) It enters the human body as sporozoites through the bite of an infected female Anopheles.\n(ii) In humans, the parasites first attack RBCs and later multiply within liver cells.\n(iii) Sporozoites are formed inside the mosquito and stored in its salivary glands.\n(iv) The human is the only host needed to complete the life cycle.\n(v) The female Anopheles mosquito acts both as a host and as a vector.\n(vi) Haemozoin is released when infected liver cells rupture.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (v) are correct. The parasite multiplies in liver cells first and then attacks RBCs, so (ii) is wrong. It needs two hosts, human and mosquito, so (iv) is wrong. Haemozoin is released on RBC rupture, not liver cell rupture, so (vi) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Fishes like Gambusia are introduced into ponds as a measure against malaria and filariasis. Why are they introduced?","image":"","options":["They feed on mosquito larvae.","They release chemicals that kill adult mosquitoes.","They feed on the eggs of filarial worms present in water.","They consume sporozoites of Plasmodium in the water."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Gambusia controls the vector by eating mosquito larvae in ponds, which reduces the mosquito population. It does not act on adult mosquitoes or directly on the parasites.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following measures:\n(i) Avoiding stagnation of water around residential areas\n(ii) Regular cleaning of household coolers\n(iii) Introducing Gambusia in ponds\n(iv) Spraying insecticides in ditches, drainage areas and swamps\n(v) Taking a full course of antibiotics\n(vi) Not sharing combs and towels\nHow many of these are aimed directly at controlling or eliminating mosquito vectors and their breeding places?","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Measures (i) to (iv) target mosquitoes or their breeding sites, so the count is four. Antibiotics are used to treat infections, and avoiding shared combs and towels prevents ringworm; neither of these controls vectors.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are carcinogens as described in NCERT?\nX-rays, gamma rays, UV rays, chemicals in tobacco smoke, $\\alpha$-interferon, histamine","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"X-rays, gamma rays, UV rays and chemicals in tobacco smoke are carcinogens, which makes four. $\\alpha$-interferon is used to treat cancer, and histamine is a chemical released in allergy, so neither is a carcinogen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The injection given to a patient bitten by a venomous snake is an example of passive immunisation.\nReason (R): The injection contains weakened snake venom that stimulates the patient's B-lymphocytes to produce antibodies.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Antivenom contains preformed antibodies against the venom, so it is passive immunisation and A is true. R is false: the patient's B-cells are not stimulated to make antibodies, because that would be slow active immunity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about AIDS is incorrect?","image":"","options":["AIDS was first reported in 1981.","The time-lag between HIV infection and appearance of AIDS symptoms is usually 5-10 years.","Anti-retroviral drugs completely eliminate HIV and cure the patient.","HIV/AIDS is not spread by mere touch, so infected persons need not be isolated from family."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Anti-retroviral drugs are only partially effective. They can prolong life but cannot cure AIDS or prevent death. All the other statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Ringworm is one of the most common infectious diseases in humans. Which of the following genera is NOT a cause of ringworm?","image":"","options":["Microsporum","Trichophyton","Epidermophyton","Entamoeba"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Ringworm is caused by fungi of the genera Microsporum, Trichophyton and Epidermophyton. Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan that causes amoebiasis, not a skin fungus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with reference to malaria:\nList-I\n(a) Sporozoites\n(b) Haemozoin\n(c) Female Anopheles\n(d) Liver cells\nList-II\n(i) Toxic substance causing chill and high fever\n(ii) Vector that transmits the parasite\n(iii) Infectious form that enters the human body\n(iv) Site of initial multiplication of the parasite in humans","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sporozoites are the infectious form injected by the mosquito, and they first multiply in liver cells. Haemozoin, released when RBCs rupture, causes the fever. The female Anopheles is the vector.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) HIV\n(B) NACO\n(C) Toxoplasma\n(D) Anti-retroviral drugs\nList-II\n(I) Parasite causing infection in immuno-deficient persons\n(II) Only partially effective; can prolong life but cannot prevent death\n(III) Enveloped virus with an RNA genome\n(IV) Organisation in India educating people about AIDS","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"HIV is a retrovirus with an envelope around its RNA genome, and NACO educates people in India about AIDS. Toxoplasma is a parasite that infects AIDS patients, and anti-retroviral drugs only prolong the patient's life.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements regarding cancer are correct?\n(a) Computed tomography uses X-rays to generate a three-dimensional image of internal organs.\n(b) MRI uses strong magnetic fields and ionising radiations to detect changes in living tissue.\n(c) UV radiation, though non-ionising, can damage DNA and cause neoplastic transformation.\n(d) α-interferon is used as a biological response modifier to activate the patient's immune system against the tumour.\n(e) Proto-oncogenes are present in normal cells and may cause oncogenic transformation when activated.\n(f) Benign tumours spread to distant sites through blood and form new tumours.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c), (d) and (e) are correct. MRI uses non-ionising radiation, so (b) is wrong. Spreading to distant sites (metastasis) is a feature of malignant tumours, not benign ones, so (f) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Ascaris\nB. Wuchereria\nC. Trichophyton\nD. Aedes\nList-II\nI. Blockage of the intestinal passage\nII. Chronic inflammation of the lymphatic vessels of the lower limbs\nIII. Dry, scaly, itchy lesions on the skin, nails and scalp\nIV. Vector of dengue and chikungunya\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ascaris, an intestinal roundworm, can block the intestinal passage. Wuchereria inflames the lymphatic vessels and causes elephantiasis. Trichophyton causes scaly, itchy ringworm lesions, and Aedes mosquitoes transmit dengue and chikungunya.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A patient has had a sustained high fever of 39–40°C for several days, along with weakness, stomach pain and loss of appetite. Which test is used to confirm the suspected disease?","image":"","options":["ELISA","Widal test","Biopsy","Histopathological study of bone marrow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"These are the symptoms of typhoid fever, which is confirmed by the Widal test. ELISA is the diagnostic test associated with AIDS, and biopsy is used for cancer detection.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"An antibody molecule is represented as $H_2L_2$. This notation indicates that each antibody has:","image":"","options":["Two heavy chains and two light chains, four peptide chains in all","Two hydrophobic and two lipid-binding domains","Two heavy chains only, each made of two light subunits","Four heavy chains joined to two light chains"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each antibody has four peptide chains: two longer heavy (H) chains and two smaller light (L) chains. This gives the notation $H_2L_2$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Cells sloughed off from a tumor travel through the blood, lodge at a distant site and start a new tumor there. This property, regarded as the most feared feature of malignant tumors, is called:","image":"","options":["Contact inhibition","Metastasis","Benign proliferation","Histopathology"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Metastasis is the spread of malignant tumor cells through the blood to distant sites, where they form new tumors. Benign tumors stay confined to their original location and do not do this.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Addiction\n(b) Dependence\n(c) Increased receptor tolerance\n(d) Excessive drug dosage\nList-II\n(i) Unpleasant withdrawal syndrome when a regular dose is abruptly stopped\n(ii) Coma and death due to respiratory failure, heart failure or cerebral hemorrhage\n(iii) Psychological attachment to effects such as euphoria and a temporary feeling of well-being\n(iv) Receptors respond only to higher doses","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Addiction is the psychological attachment to a drug's effects, while dependence is the body's tendency to show withdrawal symptoms when the drug is stopped. Higher tolerance means receptors respond only to larger doses, and overdose can cause coma and death. The common trap is swapping addiction and dependence.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A widely used diagnostic test for confirming HIV infection is:","image":"","options":["Widal test","ELISA","Biopsy with histopathology","MRI"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Enzyme linked immuno-sorbent assay (ELISA) is the widely used diagnostic test for AIDS. The Widal test is used for typhoid, while biopsy and MRI are used to detect cancer.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of innate barrier) with List-II (example):\nList-I\n(a) Physical barrier\n(b) Physiological barrier\n(c) Cellular barrier\n(d) Cytokine barrier\nList-II\n(i) Natural killer cells\n(ii) Interferons\n(iii) Skin\n(iv) Saliva in the mouth","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Skin is a physical barrier and saliva is a physiological barrier. NK cells are a cellular barrier, and interferons are a cytokine barrier. A common trap is placing saliva, a secretion, under physical barriers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, tobacco chewing is particularly associated with an increased risk of cancer of the:","image":"","options":["Urinary bladder","Oral cavity","Lung","Throat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT links tobacco chewing specifically to cancer of the oral cavity. Lung, urinary bladder and throat cancers are the ones NCERT associates with smoking.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about amoebiasis is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Houseflies act as mechanical carriers that transfer the parasite from faeces to food","Stools of the patient contain excess mucous and blood clots","The parasite is transmitted to a healthy person through the bite of an infected female mosquito","Drinking water and food contaminated by faecal matter are the main source of infection"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Amoebiasis spreads through food and water contaminated with faeces, and houseflies act as mechanical carriers. Transmission by mosquito bite applies to malaria and filariasis, not amoebiasis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (event in the life cycle of Plasmodium) with List-II (site where it occurs).\nList-I\nA. Initial multiplication after entry into humans\nB. Release of haemozoin\nC. Storage of mature sporozoites\nD. Fertilisation and further development\nList-II\nI. Salivary glands of mosquito\nII. Mosquito gut\nIII. Liver cells\nIV. Red blood cells","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sporozoites first multiply in liver cells, and haemozoin is released when infected RBCs rupture. Inside the mosquito, fertilisation and development occur in the gut, and the sporozoites formed are stored in the salivary glands.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT an example of a physiological barrier of innate immunity?","image":"","options":["Acid secreted in the stomach","Mucus coating of the epithelium lining the respiratory tract","Saliva in the mouth","Tears from the eyes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The mucus coating of epithelia traps microbes, so it is a physical barrier, like the skin. Stomach acid, saliva and tears prevent microbial growth, so they are physiological barriers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Salmonella typhi\nB. Haemophilus influenzae\nC. Rhinovirus\nD. Mary Mallon\nList-II\n(i) Alveoli of the lungs get filled with fluid\n(ii) A cook who was a carrier and spread disease through food for years\n(iii) Enters the small intestine through contaminated food and water\n(iv) Infects the nose and respiratory passage; symptoms last 3-7 days\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"S. typhi enters the small intestine through contaminated food and water. H. influenzae causes pneumonia, in which the alveoli fill with fluid. Rhinoviruses cause the common cold in the upper respiratory passage. Mary Mallon ('Typhoid Mary') was a typhoid carrier. The trap is mixing up the two respiratory infections: only pneumonia involves the alveoli.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Normal cells stop dividing uncontrollably when they come into contact with neighbouring cells. Cancer cells have lost this property. What is it called?","image":"","options":["Metastasis","Contact inhibition","Oncogenic transformation","Cell differentiation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Contact inhibition is the property by which contact with other cells inhibits a normal cell's uncontrolled growth. Cancer cells lose it and keep dividing to form tumors. Metastasis is the spread of malignant cells, not the loss of growth control.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The pathogens causing filariasis are transmitted to a healthy person through the bite of female mosquito vectors.\nStatement II: Filarial worms cause an acute, rapidly developing inflammation that remains restricted to the genital organs.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Filarial worms are indeed spread by female mosquitoes, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because the inflammation is slowly developing and chronic. It usually affects the lymphatic vessels of the lower limbs, with the genital organs also often involved.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Rhinoviruses cause the common cold. Which part of the human body do they NOT infect?","image":"","options":["Nose","Respiratory passage","Lungs","Throat region of the respiratory passage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rhinoviruses infect the nose and the respiratory passage but not the lungs. Infection of the lungs (alveoli) is characteristic of pneumonia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, health can best be defined as:","image":"","options":["Absence of any disease in the body","A state of physical fitness and high work efficiency","A state of complete physical, mental and social well-being","A balance of certain humors in the body and mind"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Health is not merely the absence of disease or physical fitness. It is complete physical, mental and social well-being. The humor balance concept is an outdated, disproved view.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a primary lymphoid organ in humans?","image":"","options":["Spleen","Thymus","Tonsils","Peyer's patches"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bone marrow and thymus are the primary lymphoid organs, where immature lymphocytes become antigen-sensitive lymphocytes. Spleen, tonsils and Peyer's patches are secondary lymphoid organs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) In severe pneumonia, the lips and finger nails may turn gray to bluish in colour.\n(b) After entering the small intestine, Salmonella typhi migrates to other organs through the blood.\n(c) A healthy person may catch a common cold by using a computer mouse contaminated with an infected person's droplets.\n(d) Pneumonia spreads only by inhaling droplets or aerosols, not by sharing glasses and utensils.\n(e) Plague and diphtheria are viral diseases of humans.\n(f) Intestinal perforation and death may occur in severe cases of typhoid.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b), (c) and (f) agree with NCERT. Statement (d) is wrong because pneumonia can also spread through shared glasses and utensils. Statement (e) is wrong because plague and diphtheria are listed as bacterial diseases, not viral ones.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Smoking can lead to oxygen deficiency in the body.\nReason (R): Smoking increases the carbon monoxide content of blood and reduces the concentration of haem-bound oxygen.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Smoking raises the CO level in blood, which lowers the amount of oxygen bound to haem. This is exactly why oxygen deficiency develops, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Allergen\n(b) Mast cells\n(c) Anti-histamine\n(d) Memory B and T-cells\nList-II\n(i) Release histamine and serotonin\n(ii) Mites in dust\n(iii) Basis of protection by vaccination\n(iv) Quickly reduces symptoms of allergy\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Dust mites are allergens, and mast cells release histamine and serotonin. Anti-histamines relieve allergy symptoms. Memory B and T-cells produced after vaccination recognise the pathogen quickly on later exposure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Opioid receptors are present only in the central nervous system.\n(ii) Cocaine has a potent stimulating action on the CNS, producing euphoria and increased energy.\n(iii) Atropa belladonna and Datura are plants with hallucinogenic properties.\n(iv) Heroin is a stimulant that speeds up body functions.\n(v) Nicotine raises blood pressure and increases heart rate.\n(vi) Tobacco chewing is associated with an increased risk of cancer of the oral cavity.\n(vii) Natural cannabinoids are obtained mainly from the roots of Cannabis sativa.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (ii), (iii), (v) and (vi) are correct. Statement (i) is wrong because opioid receptors also occur in the GI tract, and (iv) is wrong because heroin is a depressant. Statement (vii) is wrong because natural cannabinoids come from the inflorescences, not the roots.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Heroin, commonly called smack, is chemically:","image":"","options":["Diacetylmorphine","Monoacetylcodeine","Tetrahydrocannabinol","Benzoylmethylecgonine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Heroin is diacetylmorphine. It is made by acetylating morphine, which is extracted from the latex of the opium poppy. The other options are other compounds, and NCERT does not use them to describe heroin.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed by NCERT as side effects of anabolic steroid use in females?\nMasculinisation; enlargement of clitoris; deepening of voice; breast enlargement; premature baldness; abnormal menstrual cycles; reduction in size of testicles","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Four of these are female side effects: masculinisation, enlargement of clitoris, deepening of voice and abnormal menstrual cycles. Breast enlargement, premature baldness and reduction in testicle size are listed for males.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): T-lymphocytes play a role in the antibody-mediated (humoral) immune response.\nReason (R): T-lymphocytes secrete antibodies into the blood.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"T-cells do not secrete antibodies themselves, so R is false. They help B-cells produce antibodies, so they do contribute to the humoral response and A is true.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cancer detection method) with List-II (its feature).\nList-I:\n(A) Biopsy\n(B) Blood and bone marrow tests\n(C) Computed tomography (CT)\n(D) Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)\nList-II:\n(I) Uses X-rays to generate a three-dimensional image of internal organs\n(II) Uses strong magnetic fields and non-ionising radiation to detect changes in living tissue\n(III) Thin stained sections of suspected tissue are examined under a microscope\n(IV) Detect increased cell counts in leukemias\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a biopsy, stained thin sections are studied histopathologically. Blood and bone marrow tests reveal raised cell counts in leukemia. CT uses X-rays to build a 3D image, while MRI uses magnetic fields and non-ionising radiation. The usual trap is swapping the imaging bases of CT and MRI.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: HIV infection in blood transfusion patients and new-borns may occur due to poor monitoring, whereas HIV more often spreads through conscious behaviour patterns.\nStatement II: In HIV-infected cells, the viral DNA formed by reverse transcriptase stays separate in the cytoplasm and never joins the host cell's DNA.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that HIV spreads mostly through conscious behaviour, while infection of transfusion patients and new-borns can result from poor monitoring, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because the viral DNA is incorporated into the host cell's DNA and directs the cell to produce virus particles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person with AIDS suffers from infections by Mycobacterium and Toxoplasma that a healthy person could normally overcome.\nReason (R): Progeny HIV particles repeatedly attack helper T-lymphocytes, progressively decreasing their number.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Helper T cells help B cells produce antibodies and are central to acquired immunity. Their progressive loss makes the patient immuno-deficient, so infections that would otherwise be overcome now take hold. R therefore correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following organs do immature lymphocytes differentiate into antigen-sensitive lymphocytes?","image":"","options":["Spleen","Thymus","Tonsils","Peyer's patches"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The thymus and bone marrow are the primary lymphoid organs, where immature lymphocytes become antigen-sensitive. The spleen, tonsils and Peyer's patches are secondary lymphoid organs, where mature lymphocytes interact with antigens.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Rhinoviruses cause the common cold, which is spread by droplets from coughs and sneezes or by contaminated objects.\nReason (R): Rhinoviruses infect the alveoli of the lungs and fill them with fluid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rhinoviruses infect the nose and respiratory passage but not the lungs. Alveoli filling with fluid is a feature of bacterial pneumonia, caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of a component of innate immunity and its type of barrier is incorrectly matched?","image":"","options":["Acid in the stomach – Physical barrier","Saliva in the mouth – Physiological barrier","Monocytes – Cellular barrier","Interferons – Cytokine barrier"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Stomach acid is a physiological barrier, along with saliva and tears. Physical barriers are skin and the mucus coating of the epithelial linings.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed in NCERT as side effects of anabolic steroid use in males?\n(i) Acne\n(ii) Reduction in the size of the testicles\n(iii) Enlargement of the clitoris\n(iv) Breast enlargement\n(v) Deepening of voice\n(vi) Premature baldness\n(vii) Enlargement of the prostate gland\n(viii) Excessive hair growth on the face and body","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The five male side effects in the list are acne, smaller testicles, breast enlargement, premature baldness and an enlarged prostate. Clitoral enlargement, deepening of voice and excessive facial and body hair are side effects listed for females, and they reflect masculinisation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"The hepatitis B vaccine made by recombinant DNA technology is produced by expressing the antigenic polypeptide of the pathogen in:","image":"","options":["Yeast","Chick embryo cells","Human lymphocytes","Inactivated whole hepatitis B virus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In this vaccine, recombinant DNA technology is used to make the viral antigenic polypeptide in yeast. This allows large-scale production and greater availability of the vaccine. No whole virus or animal cells are needed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following can transmit HIV from an infected person to a healthy person?\n(a) Sexual contact\n(b) Hugging\n(c) Transfusion of contaminated blood\n(d) Sharing infected needles and syringes\n(e) From mother to child through the placenta\n(f) Shaking hands","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"HIV spreads only through body fluids, so routes (a), (c), (d) and (e) can transmit it. Hugging and shaking hands are mere touch or physical contact, which does not spread HIV.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy person most commonly acquires ascariasis by:","image":"","options":["Bite of an infected female mosquito","Inhaling droplets released by an infected person","Consuming water, vegetables or fruits contaminated with eggs of the parasite","Using the towel or comb of an infected person"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ascaris eggs pass out in the faeces of infected persons and contaminate soil, water and plants. Infection follows when such contaminated water, vegetables or fruits are consumed. Mosquito bite is the route for filariasis, and sharing towels or combs spreads ringworm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Prevention of amoebiasis depends mainly on hygiene of drinking water and food, whereas prevention of malaria depends mainly on controlling mosquitoes and their breeding places.\nStatement II: Houseflies in amoebiasis act as vectors in which the parasite develops, just as Plasmodium develops in Anopheles.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Amoebiasis spreads through faecally contaminated food and water, while malaria is controlled by eliminating vectors, so Statement I is correct. Houseflies are only mechanical carriers, and no development of Entamoeba occurs in them, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the human immune system is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The immune system consists of lymphoid organs, tissues, cells and soluble molecules such as antibodies","Immature lymphocytes differentiate into antigen-sensitive lymphocytes in the spleen and lymph nodes","The spleen mainly contains lymphocytes and phagocytes","Antigens trapped in lymph nodes activate the lymphocytes present there and cause an immune response"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Immature lymphocytes become antigen-sensitive in the primary lymphoid organs, the bone marrow and thymus. The spleen and lymph nodes are secondary organs that receive already-matured lymphocytes. The other three statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (component of innate immunity) with List-II (type of barrier).\nList-I\nA. Mucus lining the respiratory tract\nB. Tears from the eyes\nC. Natural killer cells\nD. Interferons\nList-II\nI. Cellular barrier\nII. Cytokine barrier\nIII. Physical barrier\nIV. Physiological barrier\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mucus coating traps microbes (physical), tears prevent microbial growth (physiological), NK cells destroy microbes (cellular) and interferons protect uninfected cells (cytokine). Mucus and tears are easily confused, but mucus works as a physical trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Opioids exert their effects by binding to specific opioid receptors present in the:","image":"","options":["Heart and blood vessels only","Liver and kidneys","Adrenal medulla and pancreas","Central nervous system and gastrointestinal tract"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Opioid receptors are located in the central nervous system and the gastrointestinal tract. Do not confuse this with cannabinoids, which are known for their effects on the cardiovascular system.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which deadly infectious disease has been completely eradicated through the use of vaccines and immunisation programmes?","image":"","options":["Polio","Smallpox","Tetanus","Diphtheria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Smallpox has been completely eradicated by vaccination. Polio, diphtheria, pneumonia and tetanus have only been controlled to a large extent, not eradicated.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pathogen) with List-II (disease caused):\nList-I\nA. Salmonella typhi\nB. Wuchereria bancrofti\nC. Trichophyton\nD. Rhinovirus\nList-II\nI. Common cold\nII. Ringworm\nIII. Typhoid\nIV. Elephantiasis\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Salmonella typhi causes typhoid. Wuchereria, the filarial worm, causes elephantiasis. Trichophyton is a ringworm fungus. Rhinoviruses cause the common cold.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are symptoms of amoebiasis as described in NCERT?\nConstipation\nAbdominal pain and cramps\nStools with excess mucus\nBlood clots in stools\nSustained high fever of 39° to 40°C\nLips and finger nails turning bluish","image":"","options":["Three","Five","Six","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The first four are symptoms of amoebiasis. Sustained high fever of 39° to 40°C is characteristic of typhoid. Bluish lips and nails occur in severe pneumonia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about immunisation is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Active immunity can be induced by exposure to living or dead microbes or even other proteins acting as antigens","A vaccine works by introducing preformed antibodies that directly neutralise the pathogen, so no memory cells are formed","Passive immunisation is preferred when a quick immune response is needed, as in tetanus","Vaccines produced by recombinant DNA technology allow large-scale production and greater availability"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A vaccine contains antigenic proteins or an inactivated/weakened pathogen, not antibodies. It stimulates the body's own antibody production and generates memory B and T-cells. Supplying preformed antibodies describes passive immunisation, which is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a genus of fungi responsible for ringworm in humans?","image":"","options":["Microsporum","Trichophyton","Epidermophyton","Wuchereria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Ringworm is caused by fungi of the genera Microsporum, Trichophyton and Epidermophyton. Wuchereria is a filarial worm (a helminth) that causes elephantiasis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which enzyme allows the RNA genome of HIV to form viral DNA inside the host cell?","image":"","options":["DNA ligase","RNA polymerase","Reverse transcriptase","Restriction endonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"HIV is a retrovirus. Its enzyme reverse transcriptase copies the viral RNA genome into DNA, which then joins the host DNA.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Patient X develops chronic damage to the joints because her immune system attacks her own tissues. Patient Y starts sneezing and wheezing on visiting a place with abundant pollen, and the symptoms disappear on leaving. Which option correctly explains both cases?","image":"","options":["X has an allergy mediated by IgE; Y has an auto-immune disease caused by failure to recognise self","X has an auto-immune disease due to a failure to distinguish self from non-self; Y shows an exaggerated IgE-based response involving histamine release from mast cells","Both X and Y are immunodeficient, and their helper T-cell counts are reduced","X needs anti-histamines for relief, while Y needs a vaccine containing pollen antibodies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"X fits auto-immunity (as in rheumatoid arthritis), where the body attacks self-cells. Y fits allergy, an exaggerated IgE response to an environmental antigen, with mast cells releasing histamine and serotonin. The distractors swap the two or confuse them with immunodeficiency.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A virus infects the nose and respiratory passage but does not infect the lungs. It causes nasal congestion, sore throat and hoarseness that last 3-7 days. Which pathogen fits this description?","image":"","options":["Haemophilus influenzae","Rhino virus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Human Immunodeficiency Virus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rhino viruses cause the common cold. They infect the nose and respiratory passage but spare the lungs. H. influenzae and S. pneumoniae are bacteria that infect the alveoli and cause pneumonia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Substance)\nA. Morphine\nB. Cocaine\nC. Cannabinoids\nD. Nicotine\nList-II (Feature)\n(i) Interferes with transport of the neurotransmitter dopamine\n(ii) Stimulates adrenal gland to release adrenaline and noradrenaline\n(iii) Very effective sedative and painkiller used after surgery\n(iv) Known for effects on the cardiovascular system\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each substance pairs with one feature:\n- Morphine is a sedative and painkiller used in post-surgical patients.\n- Cocaine interferes with dopamine transport.\n- Cannabinoids are known for cardiovascular effects.\n- Nicotine triggers adrenaline and noradrenaline release from the adrenal gland.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs, where immature lymphocytes differentiate into antigen-sensitive lymphocytes.\nStatement II: Spleen is a primary lymphoid organ that also acts as a reservoir of erythrocytes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The spleen does filter blood and store erythrocytes. However, it is a secondary lymphoid organ, so Statement II is incorrect, while Statement I is correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed in NCERT as cellular barriers of innate immunity?\nNeutrophils (PMNL), monocytes, natural killer cells, macrophages in tissues, B-lymphocytes, T-lymphocytes, interferons, mucus","image":"","options":["3","6","5","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Only neutrophils, monocytes, natural killer cells and macrophages are cellular barriers, so the answer is 4. B and T lymphocytes belong to acquired immunity, interferons are a cytokine barrier and mucus is a physical barrier.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A boy who has never been vaccinated against tetanus gets a deep wound and is given an injection of tetanus antitoxin. Several months later he suffers another tetanus-prone injury. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["The antitoxin gave him immediate protection but did not generate memory B and T cells, so it does not ensure a rapid secondary response later; active immunisation is needed for long-term protection.","The antitoxin stimulated his B-lymphocytes to produce antibodies, so the second exposure will trigger a highly intensified anamnestic response.","The antitoxin acted as an antigen and generated only memory T-cells, which will secrete antibodies on the second exposure.","The antitoxin provided slow protection that took weeks to develop, but this protection is now permanent."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Antitoxin supplies ready-made antibodies (passive immunity), so protection is immediate but memory cells are not formed. Memory B and T cells, the basis of a rapid secondary response, are produced only when the body meets antigens itself, as in vaccination. Also, T-cells do not secrete antibodies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cancer-causing genes is correct?","image":"","options":["Proto-oncogenes are found only in cancer cells and are absent from normal cells","Cellular oncogenes (c-onc) occur in normal cells and, when activated under certain conditions, can lead to oncogenic transformation","Viral oncogenes are normal human genes that are carried by every virus","Proto-oncogenes are the genes carried by oncogenic viruses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that cellular oncogenes (proto-oncogenes, c-onc) are present in normal cells and can cause cancer when activated. Viral oncogenes are carried by oncogenic viruses; they are not normal human genes and are not carried by all viruses.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Natural cannabinoids are obtained from the inflorescences of Cannabis sativa, and its flower tops, leaves and resin are used to produce marijuana, hashish, charas and ganja.\nStatement II: Cannabinoids act principally on receptors present in the gastrointestinal tract and are usually taken by injection.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I matches NCERT. Statement II is wrong on two counts: cannabinoid receptors are present principally in the brain, and these drugs are generally taken by inhalation or oral ingestion, not injection.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The thymus is quite large at the time of birth but becomes very small by the time puberty is attained.\nReason (R): The thymus is a secondary lymphoid organ where mature lymphocytes interact with antigens and proliferate into effector cells.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The thymus does shrink progressively with age until puberty, so A is true. R is false because the thymus is a primary lymphoid organ; antigen interaction and proliferation into effector cells occur in secondary lymphoid organs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"The following events occur after HIV enters a human body:\n(P) Viral DNA gets incorporated into the host cell's DNA\n(Q) Reverse transcriptase forms viral DNA from the RNA genome\n(R) Progeny viruses released into blood attack other helper T-lymphocytes\n(S) The virus enters macrophages\n(T) The person suffers infections by Mycobacterium and parasites like Toxoplasma\nWhich is the correct sequence?","image":"","options":["Q → S → P → R → T","S → Q → P → R → T","S → P → Q → R → T","S → Q → P → T → R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"HIV first enters macrophages, where reverse transcriptase copies its RNA into DNA. That DNA integrates into the host genome. Progeny viruses then repeatedly attack helper T-cells, and only after TH numbers fall do opportunistic infections such as Mycobacterium and Toxoplasma appear.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A malaria patient experiences chill and high fever that recur every three to four days.\nReason (R): When RBCs rupture, a toxic substance called haemozoin is released into the blood.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Plasmodium multiplies inside RBCs and ruptures them, which releases haemozoin. This toxin is responsible for the recurring chill and high fever. So R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Innate immunity is a non-specific type of defence that is present at the time of birth.\nStatement II: Acquired immunity is pathogen specific but does not show memory of earlier encounters.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Innate immunity is non-specific and present from birth, so Statement I is correct. Acquired immunity is pathogen specific and is characterised by memory, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Cocaine, commonly called coke or crack, is obtained from:","image":"","options":["Papaver somniferum","Cannabis sativa","Erythroxylum coca","Atropa belladonna"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cocaine is a coca alkaloid from Erythroxylum coca, a plant native to South America. Papaver gives morphine and Cannabis gives cannabinoids. Atropa belladonna is cited only as a hallucinogenic plant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Persons who take drugs intravenously and share needles and syringes are much more likely to acquire which pair of serious chronic infections?","image":"","options":["Typhoid and cholera","AIDS and Hepatitis B","Malaria and filariasis","Pneumonia and common cold"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The viruses causing AIDS and Hepatitis B pass from person to person through infected needles and syringes. Both can also be transmitted through sexual contact or infected blood.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Heroin is a white, odourless, bitter crystalline compound obtained by acetylation of morphine","Heroin is a depressant that slows down body functions and is generally taken by snorting and injection","Cocaine interferes with dopamine transport and acts as a depressant of the central nervous system","Atropa belladonna and Datura are plants with hallucinogenic properties"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cocaine does interfere with dopamine transport, but it is a potent CNS stimulant that produces euphoria and increased energy, not a depressant. The depressant among these drugs is heroin.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pathogen) with List-II (main site of infection described in NCERT).\nList-I\nA. Salmonella typhi\nB. Streptococcus pneumoniae\nC. Rhinovirus\nD. Entamoeba histolytica\nList-II\nI. Large intestine\nII. Alveoli of lungs\nIII. Nose and respiratory passage\nIV. Small intestine, from where it migrates to other organs through blood","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"S. typhi enters the small intestine and spreads through blood. S. pneumoniae infects the alveoli, and rhinoviruses infect the nose and respiratory passage but not the lungs. E. histolytica lives in the large intestine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"In a diagram showing the lymphoid organs of the human body, a lobed organ lies near the heart, beneath the breastbone. This organ is the:","image":"","options":["Spleen","Thymus","Lymph node","Tonsil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The thymus is a lobed organ located near the heart and beneath the breastbone (sternum). The spleen is a bean-shaped organ and is not described at this position.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are examples of passive immunity?\n(i) IgA received by an infant through colostrum\n(ii) Antibodies passing from mother to foetus through the placenta\n(iii) Antitoxin injected into a person infected with tetanus\n(iv) Antivenom injected after a snakebite\n(v) Hepatitis B vaccine produced from yeast\n(vi) Protection developed after recovering from a natural infection","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Items (i) to (iv) all involve ready-made antibodies, so they are passive immunity. A vaccine and a natural infection both make the body produce its own antibodies, which is active immunity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about HIV and AIDS:\n(a) HIV is an enveloped retrovirus with a DNA genome.\n(b) Infected macrophages keep producing virus and act like an HIV factory.\n(c) Progeny viruses repeatedly attack helper T-lymphocytes, progressively decreasing their number.\n(d) ELISA is a widely used diagnostic test for AIDS.\n(e) Anti-retroviral drugs completely cure the infection if started early.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(b), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(b), (c), (d) and (e) only","(a), (d) and (e) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"HIV has an RNA genome, which reverse transcriptase copies into viral DNA, so (a) is false. Anti-retroviral drugs only prolong life and cannot prevent death, so (e) is false. Statements (b), (c) and (d) agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of the Plasmodium life cycle shows events in two hosts. Which of the following statements about this cycle is correct?","image":"","options":["In humans, the parasites first multiply within RBCs and then move to liver cells","Haemozoin released by rupture of liver cells causes the chill and fever recurring every three to four days","Sporozoites are formed within the mosquito and stored in its salivary glands; the female Anopheles acts as both host and vector","Both male and female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit sporozoites to humans while feeding"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sporozoites develop in the female Anopheles and are stored in its salivary glands, so the mosquito is both a host and the vector. In humans, multiplication occurs first in liver cells and then in RBCs. Haemozoin comes from ruptured RBCs, not liver cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Malignant malaria, the most serious and potentially fatal type of malaria, is caused by","image":"","options":["Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium malariae","Wuchereria bancrofti"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Of the Plasmodium species, P. falciparum causes malignant malaria, which can be fatal. Wuchereria is a filarial worm that causes elephantiasis, not malaria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pathogen) with List-II (disease) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(a) Haemophilus influenzae\n(b) Entamoeba histolytica\n(c) Wuchereria bancrofti\n(d) Trichophyton\nList-II:\n(i) Ringworm\n(ii) Filariasis\n(iii) Pneumonia\n(iv) Amoebiasis","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Haemophilus influenzae is a bacterium that causes pneumonia, and Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan that causes amoebiasis. Wuchereria is the filarial worm, and Trichophyton is one of the fungi that cause ringworm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The mental state of a person can affect his/her health.\nReason (R): The mind influences the immune system through the neural and endocrine systems, and the immune system maintains our health.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The mind acts on the immune system through the neural and endocrine systems. Since the immune system maintains health, the mental state can influence health. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are described in NCERT as drugs normally used as medicines (for conditions such as depression, insomnia or post-surgical pain) that are often abused?\nBarbiturates, Amphetamines, Benzodiazepines, Morphine, Cocaine, Hashish","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Four qualify. Barbiturates, amphetamines and benzodiazepines are medicines for mental illness that are often abused, and morphine is a valuable painkiller after surgery. NCERT gives no medicinal role for cocaine or hashish.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are secondary lymphoid organs?\nSpleen, Thymus, Tonsils, Bone marrow, Appendix, Peyer's patches, Lymph nodes","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Seven"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Spleen, tonsils, appendix, Peyer's patches and lymph nodes are secondary lymphoid organs, giving five. Thymus and bone marrow are primary lymphoid organs, so counting all seven is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"In which form does Plasmodium enter the human body when an infected mosquito bites?","image":"","options":["Gametocytes","Sporozoites","Merozoites released from RBCs","Cysts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Plasmodium enters humans as sporozoites, the infectious form, through the bite of an infected female Anopheles. Gametocytes are the stage the mosquito takes up from human blood, not the stage it injects.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events after HIV enters the human body in the correct sequence:\n(i) Viral DNA gets incorporated into the host cell's DNA\n(ii) Virus enters macrophages\n(iii) Number of helper T-lymphocytes progressively decreases\n(iv) Viral RNA is copied into DNA by reverse transcriptase\n(v) Patient suffers from infections that would otherwise be overcome\n(vi) Infected cells produce virus particles that attack helper T-lymphocytes","image":"","options":["(ii) → (i) → (iv) → (vi) → (iii) → (v)","(ii) → (iv) → (i) → (vi) → (iii) → (v)","(iv) → (ii) → (i) → (iii) → (vi) → (v)","(ii) → (iv) → (i) → (iii) → (vi) → (v)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"HIV enters macrophages, reverse-transcribes its RNA into DNA, and integrates that DNA into the host genome. Virus particles are then produced, which attack helper T cells and reduce their number, leading to infections. The common trap is placing integration before reverse transcription, but RNA cannot integrate into DNA directly.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the measures suggested for preventing and controlling drug and alcohol abuse among adolescents?","image":"","options":["Educating and counselling the child to accept disappointments and failures as part of life","Seeking help from parents, peers and close trusted friends","Pushing the child to perform beyond his/her threshold limits so that no free time is left","Channelising the child's energy into sports, reading, music and yoga"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says a child should not be pushed unduly to perform beyond his/her threshold limits, because such undue pressure is itself a risk factor. The other three options are measures NCERT recommends.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HEALTH-DISEASE-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Health and Disease","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Health and Disease","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Ascaris\nB. Wuchereria\nC. Trichophyton\nD. Aedes\nList-II\nI. Vector of dengue and chikungunya\nII. Dry, scaly, intensely itchy lesions on skin, nails and scalp\nIII. Chronic inflammation of lymphatic vessels of lower limbs\nIV. Internal bleeding, anaemia and blockage of intestinal passage\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ascaris causes internal bleeding, anaemia and intestinal blockage. Wuchereria inflames the lymphatic vessels of the lower limbs. Trichophyton produces the itchy, scaly lesions of ringworm, and Aedes mosquitoes transmit dengue and chikungunya.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 7 · Human Health and Disease","difficulty":"medium"}]});
